          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Einstein-Hilbert action
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            that defines the dynamics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"} with boundary
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,g)
            the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian manifold
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            a (pseudo-)Riemannian metric on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %strong vacuum Einstein-Hilbert action
            is the number
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dvol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g \;\mapsto\; \int_X R(g) dvol(g)
                  \\,,
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(g) : X\to \mathbb{R}
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+curvature"} scalar curvature
                function of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi dvol
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dvol(g)
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
                defined by the metric
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_X
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integration of differential forms
                over
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            If the metric is instead encoded in terms of an
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+group"} Poincare group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+formulation+of+gravity"} first-order formulation of gravity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim X = 4
            and assuming for simplicity that the underlying bundle is trivial) this is equivalently
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi R
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi e
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \;\mapsto\; \int_X \langle R \wedge e \wedge e \rangle
                  \\,,
          %p where now
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                denotes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
                2-form of the
                = succeed "-connection" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+group"} orthogonal group
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∇
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                denotes the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -\rangle
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
                which in terms of the canonical basis has components
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                            %mi c
                            %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\epsilon_{a b c d})
          %p In either case this assignment defines a function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi EH
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Met
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{EH} \;\colon\; Met(X) \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            on the space of (pseudo-)Riemannian metrics on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                Einstein-Hilbert
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
          %p
            This functional is invariant under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Bertschinger02"} Bertschinger 02, section 4.2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Met
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Diff
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Met(X)//Diff(X)
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"} general covariance
          %p
            More generally, this extends to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for gravity coupled to “matter” (which in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            conventionally means every other field, for instance the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi EH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi d
                  %mi vol
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (g,\phi) \;\mapsto\; \int_X (L_{EH}(g) + L_\phi(g)) d vol(g)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+point"} critical point
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            with underlying manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            (the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi EH
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta S_{EH}(g) = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            To derive the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equation"} Einstein Field equations
            from this, we will look at the Einstein-Hilbert action action in its usual form (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            , the speed of light, is set to unity), in spacetime with no matter fields (i.e., matter Lagrangian
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℒ
                      %mi M
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{L}_M=0
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 16
                      %mi π
                      %mi G
                  %mo ∫
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msqrt
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mn 4
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S = \frac{1}{16\pi G} \int \operatorname{tr}_g(R) \sqrt{-g} \, \mathrm{d}^4x \;,
          %p The variation of this action can be performed:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi δ
                      %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\delta S}{\delta g}=0
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Einstein%E2%80%93Hilbert_action"} Wikipedia Page
            shows how to vary the action (with matter fields) through components, giving us the famed
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equation"} Einstein field equations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo −
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                  %mi g
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} tr
                    %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R-\frac{1}{2}g\,\operatorname{tr}_g(R)=T
          %p
            Here,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stress-energy+tensor"} energy-momentum tensor
          %p (…)
          %h2#History History
          %p
            Historically, Einstein had been working towards what today are called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            for some time. What is today called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+tensor"} Einstein tensor
            in the equation had been missing the trace term in one of his first publications, and other things remained unclear. Einstein was working with the 10 coordinate components of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensors"} tensors
            and trying to make the theory match classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newtonian+physics"} Newtonian physics
            in the right limit. The “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+covariance"}> general covariance
            ” of the equations made this a subtle problem, at least back in the times when these issues were just beginning to be appreciated.
          %p
            Hilbert had been following these developments and had listened to Einstein talk about them. Apparently at some point Hilbert, who believed in the axiomatic foundation of physics (as is evident from
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+sixth+problem"} Hilbert's sixth problem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+principle"} action principle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+points"} critical points
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            on the space of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metrics"} pseudo-Riemannian metrics
            on some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
            properties is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+scalar+curvature"} Riemann scalar curvature
            times the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            of the given metric (hence the “Einstein-Hilbert action”). He computed the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational derivative
            of this and thereby found
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            apparently around the same time if not a little bit before Einstein published the final version of his theory.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#SauerMajer"}> Sauer-Majer, from p. 402
            ) a talk of Hilbert is reproduced that reviews Einstein’s work, from the point of view of Hilbert’s having known the right answer all along from the variational principle (hence from the axioms) as Einstein struggled (until a few days before the lecture was given!) with finding the right equations of gravity. This comment culminates (
            %a{:href => "#SauerMajer"}> Sauer-Majer, from p. 405
            ) with the curious remark, paraphrasing just a little: that the fact that Einstein after his “colossal detour” ends up with the same equations of motion as Hilbert already had is a “nice consistency check” (
            %em> schöne Gewähr
            )
          %p p. 402:
          %blockquote
            %p Das von mir aufgestellte Hamiltonsche Prinzip lautet
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi I
                    %msqrt
                      %mi g
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 3
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 4
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \int \int \int \int I \sqrt{g} \, d x^1 d x^2 d x^3 d x^4 = 0
          %p
            (this defines the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+point"} critical point
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+extremal+action"} principle of extremal action
          %p p. 404:
          %blockquote
            %p Indess mündet das Schlussresultat der letzten Einsteinschen Untersuchung wieder auf ein Hamiltonsches Prinzip, das dem ursprünglich von mir aufgestellten gleicht; ja es könnte sein, dass diese Einsteinsche Theorie inhaltlich sich mit der von mir ursprünglich aufgestellten Theorie völlig deckt.
          %p p. 405:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Als Entgelt dafür können wir gegenwärtig als sicher ansehen, dass die oben von mir aufgestellten Gl. gewiss den
              %em Grundstock für die Weltgl.
              ausmachen. In einer letzten vor wenigen Tagen erschienenen Note gelangt Einstein zu dem Hamiltonschen Prinzip:
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo ∫
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi K
                    %mo +
                    %mi α
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo +
                    %mi β
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %msqrt
                      %mi g
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 3
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 4
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta \int \int \int \int  \{K + \alpha \Phi + \beta \phi\} \sqrt{g} d x^1 d x^2 d x^3 d x^4 = 0
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
            %p Und wenn auf dem kollossalen Umweg über Levi Civita, Weyl, Schouten, Eddington Einst. zu dem Resultat zurückgelangt, so liegt darin sicher eine schöne Gewähr.
          %p In Constance Reid’s biography of Hilbert, one finds in Chapter XVII the following:
          %blockquote
            %p
              In Göttingen, in the weekly Hilbert-Debye seminars, it seemed to those few students who were left
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              due to WWI
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              that the “living pulse” of physical research was at their finger tips. The work of Einstein as he pressed forward toward a general theory of relativity was followed with great interest. Also followed was the work of others who were trying to reach the same goal. Hilbert was especially fascinated by the ideas of Gustav Mie, then in Greifswald, who was attempting to develop a theory of matter on the fundamentals of the relativity principle; and in his own investigations he was able to bring together Mie’s program of pure field theory and Einstein’s theory of gravitation. At the same time, while Einstein was attempting in a rather roundabout way to develop the binding laws for the 10 coefficients of the differential form which determines gravitation, Hilbert independantly solved the problem in a different, more direct way. Both men arrived at almost the same time at the same goal. As the western front settled down for the winter, Einstein presented his two papers, “On general relativity theory” to the Berlin Academy on November 11 and 25; Hilbert presented his first note on “The foundations of physics” to the Royal Society of Science in Gottingen on November 20, 1915. It was a remarkable coincidence … but even more remarkable, according to
              = succeed "Max" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              Born (who was now in Berlin with Einstein), was the fact that it led, not to a controversy over priority, but to a series of friendly encounters and letters. Hilbert freely admitted, and frequently stated in lectures, that the great idea was Einstein’s.
          %p
            More citations on the priority issue are listed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://physics.stackexchange.com/a/56893/5603"} this Physics.SE comment
          %p
            The success of the Einstein-Hilbert
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+principle"} action principle
            in the formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            probably had a considerable influence on the theoretical physics to follow and led some physcist to explore more systematically the space of action functionals invariant under prescribed symmetries. This way notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            variant of Einstein-gravity in the context of Riemannian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            was found, a theory which eventually leads to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring+theory"} superstring theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theories
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenological physics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Maxwell+theory"} Einstein-Maxwell theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac+theory"} Einstein-Yang-Mills-Dirac theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein-Maxwell-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs+theory"} Einstein-Maxwell-Yang-Mills-Dirac-Higgs theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+order+curvature+corrections"} higher order curvature corrections
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
